1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 b6 4. a3 Bb7 5. Nc3 d5 6. cxd5 exd5 7. Bg5 Be7 8. e3
O-O 9. Bd3 Nbd7 {(This line of the a3 Queen's Indian is supposed innocuous)}
10. O-O Ne4 11. Bxe7 Qxe7 12. Qb3 Ndf6 13. Rad1 Rfd8 14. Ne5 c5 15. Bb1 c4 16.
Qc2 Nxc3 17. bxc3 $1 Qxa3 18. f3 Qd6 {(Bradbury has sacrificed a pawn for an
attack which I completely underestimated at the time. He prepares e4, e5, so
it is difficult to find the time to advance the queen-side pawns)} 19. Rde1 g6
20. e4 Nh5 {(White was threatening 21.Nxf7 followed by 22. e5. But now
Bradbury manoeuvres his Q to a very strong position)} 21. Qc1 $1 b5 22. g4 Ng7
23. Qh6 Re8 24. f4 dxe4 25. Re3 {(The threat is 26.Rh3. Desperate times call
for desperate measures)} Rxe5 26. fxe5 Qe6 27. Rf4 (27. Qh4 $5) 27... a5 (27...
b4 $5) 28. Bxe4 (28. Rh3 {is still not possible because of} Nh5 $1 29. gxh5
Qxh3 30. Rxf7 Qg4+ 31. Kf2 Kxf7 $19) ({Stockfish 10 finds} 28. Kg2 $1 {with
the idea of Rh3, when Black won't have the defence ...Nh5 as White can simply
capture with gxh5 - JS, 2018}) 28... Bxe4 29. Rfxe4 b4 30. Rh3 Nh5 31. Rh4 b3
32. gxh5 b2 {(White has an extra rook and a strong attack but he must
temporarily defend with ...)} 33. Re1 a4 34. h3 $2 (34. Qf4 {is what he should
have played according to my computer:} a3 35. Qe4 Rb8 36. Qb1 {but it is hard
to put the Q on such a square in time trouble. Moreover, even here, it is by
no means over for Black. I suggest} Qa6 37. Qa2 Qb5 38. Rb1 Qb3 39. Qxb3 Rxb3
$17) ({Stockfish 10 prefers} 34. Rf4 {- JS, 2018}) 34... a3 35. hxg6 Qxg6+ 36.
Rg4 a2 {[#](The 7th Cavalry has arrived. White is lost)} 37. e6 b1=Q 38. Rxg6+
Qxg6+ $2 (38... fxg6 {which wins on the spot, was missed in huge time trouble.
The moved played however does not spoil anything. The rook ending is
completely won}) 39. Qxg6+ fxg6 40. Ra1 Kf8 41. Kf2 Ke7 42. Ke3 (42. d5 Ra5 43.
Ke3 (43. h4 h5 44. Ke3 Rxd5 45. Rxa2 Rd3+ 46. Ke4 Kxe6 47. Ra6+ Kf7 $19) 43...
Rxd5 44. Rxa2 Rd3+) 42... Kxe6 43. Ke4 Ra3 44. d5+ Kd6 45. Kd4 Ra4 46. Ke4 g5
47. Kd4 h5 48. Ke4 g4 (48... g4 49. hxg4 hxg4 50. Kf4 Kxd5 51. Rd1+ Kc5 52. Ra1
Ra3 53. Kxg4 Rxc3 54. Rxa2 Re3 {cuts the White K off from the P}) 0-1

