1. e4 e5 2. f4 {(After this move, which gives up a pawn and weakens the K,
Black has a slight advantage. I believe however that White can equalize if he
plays carefully.)} exf4 3. Qe2 {(White indulges himself a little more in the
opening. I have seen 3.Qf3, but this was new to me.)} Be7 4. d4 Nc6 5. c3 Nf6
6. Bxf4 d5 7. e5 Ne4 8. Nd2 Bf5 9. Ngf3 O-O {(Black stands fine, but White is
in the process of equalizing.)} 10. Nxe4 Bxe4 11. Nd2 Bg6 12. g3 f6 13. exf6
Bxf6 14. O-O-O Qd7 15. Bg2 $4 {(This move came as a pleasant surprise, During
the game I thought I stood a little better, but later analysis convinced me
that 15.Nb3! would be level. The point is that White must be able to meet ...
Qf5 with Qd3.)[#]} Qf5 (15... Qf5 16. Nb3 Qb1+ 17. Kd2 Qxb2+ 18. Ke1 Rae8 19.
Be5 Bxe5 $1 $19) 0-1

