1. e4 e6 2. d3 d5 3. Nd2 Nf6 4. Ngf3 dxe4 5. dxe4 Bc5 {(Black is threatening ..
.Ng4. Maybe 6.Bc4 is the right move here, but I did not see his next)} 6. Bd3
e5 $1 (6... Nc6 $2 {was what I wanted him to play and something like} 7. e5 Ng4
8. Ne4 $1 {; 6...e5 stops everything and makes the B on d3 look stupid}) 7. O-O
(7. Nxe5 $2 Qd4) (7. Nc4 {is what I should play:} Nc6 8. c3 {hoping to gain
some space on the queenside; instead I start to drift}) 7... Nc6 8. Nc4 Qe7 9.
c3 a5 {(It's amazing how quickly White now gets a bad game.)} 10. Qe2 O-O 11.
Bg5 h6 12. Bh4 $2 g5 $1 13. Bg3 Nh5 14. Ne3 Bxe3 $1 15. Qxe3 Nf4 16. Bc2 $2 b6
$1 17. Rfe1 Qf6 18. Rad1 Ne7 {(Cubitt has been playing carefully and rather
well. His plan of 16...b6 to stop Qc5, then ...Qf6 with the intention of ...
Ne7 and Ng6 further strengthening his position gave me a panic attack. I felt
I had to do something.)} 19. Nd4 $2 {(It's time to start playing like Tal! 
[why change the habits of a life-time? - ed] If Black takes the N, I retake
with the P intending e5 and then mate on h7. "If Misha has an open file 
[diagonal?] he will deliver mate" (Koblents) blah, blah.)} exd4 {(He took it!!)
} 20. cxd4 Bb7 $1 {(Not too difficult really. From now on White's game goes
rapidly downhill)} 21. d5 (21. e5 Qg7 (21... Qc6 {After the game a rather
terse Clive Cubitt pointed out that this is also OK}) 22. Bxf4 gxf4 23. Qxf4
Qxg2# {is mate}) 21... Qe5 22. Bb3 Neg6 23. d6 Rad8 $1 24. dxc7 Rxd1 25. Rxd1
Qxc7 26. h3 Re8 27. Rd4 (27. f3 Nxh3+) 27... Qc6 28. f3 Qc5 {[#](Black has
only one major problem. His position is so good, it's difficult to think of
ways to improve it. He now threatens 29...Qxd4 or 29...Ne2+. Surely it's time
for White to resign? It's sad to say however that there are some people who
just want to drag things out.)} 29. Kh2 $1 Ne2 $2 30. Bxf7+ Kxf7 31. Rd7+ Re7
32. Rxe7+ Kxe7 33. Qxe2 {(He finally went wrong. 29...Bc6 was correct. But
Black is still winning and has only four moves to get to the time control. He
still needs to find one more bad idea.)} h5 $2 {(And here it is! He weakens g5.
Bringing the B round to e6 looks better)} 34. Qd2 $1 h4 35. Bb8 Nf8 $2 (35...
Nh8 $5 $17 {Fritz4}) 36. e5 $1 {(Ouch! Threatening Bd6+ and Qxg5+. White is
now winning)} Ke6 37. Qxg5 Qf2 (37... Qe7 38. Qg4+ Kf7 39. Bd6 Qd8 40. f4 {
in preference to this lingering agony, Cubitt thows himself on his sword}) 38.
Qg8+ Kd7 39. Qf7+ Kc6 40. e6 $1 Nxe6 41. Qxe6+ Kb5 42. a4+ Kc5 43. Bc7 $1 Qxb2
44. Bd6+ Kd4 45. Be5+ {(My best swindle of recent times)} 1-0

